    <content>
      <Value><![CDATA[# Why Markdown for Blog Content?

When designing the Blog Post document type, I had to choose: Markdown editor or Rich Text (WYSIWYG)?

## The Markdown Advantage

**Simplicity for Authors**: Markdown uses intuitive syntax. `# Title` for headings, `**bold**` for emphasis.

**Version Control Friendly**: If blog content were stored as HTML, version control diffs would be messy. Markdown diffs are readable.

**Consistency**: WYSIWYG editors often produce inconsistent HTML. Markdown ensures predictable output.

## How Umbraco Handles It

Here's the magic: Umbraco's Markdown editor automatically converts Markdown to HTML during rendering. In my template, I just use:

```
@Html.Raw(Model.Value("content"))
```

That's it. No manual conversion. No Markdig library needed. Umbraco built this in.

## A Lesson in Developer Experience

Umbraco could have given me raw Markdown and forced me to render it. Instead, the system does the right thing automatically. That's good API design.

## The Trade-off

Non-technical authors might find Markdown intimidating. WYSIWYG editors are easier for them. The choice depends on your audience.

For a developer conference demo? Markdown is perfect.]]></Value>
    </content>
    <excerpt>
      <Value><![CDATA[I chose Markdown for blog content. Here's why that choice matters and how Umbraco makes the conversion seamless.]]></Value>
